      "role": "user",
      "content": "Consider the potential energy graph for the reaction shown below, with levels P, Q, and R for potential energy (kJ·mol⁻¹) against the course of reaction.\n\nThe activation energy for the FORWARD reaction in terms of P, Q and R is:\nA. Q\nB. R − P\nC. Q − R\nD. Q − P"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner is stuck, so begin with the key idea without giving the option: forward activation energy is measured from the reactants up to the peak. Ask one focused diagnostic question.",
      "content": "For a forward reaction, activation energy is the energy difference from the reactants to the top of the energy barrier. Which level represents the reactants, and which represents the peak?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I think R is the reactants and Q is the peak, so maybe Q − R?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner correctly identifies Q as the peak but confuses the reactants with the products. Clarify that P is the forward reactant level in this graph, then show the required subtraction and match the memo.",
      "content": "You’ve identified the peak as Q; for the forward reaction, the reactants are at P. So subtract the reactant energy from the peak energy:\n\n**Final Answer: D. $Q - P$**\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $E_{a,\\text{forward}} = E_{\\text{peak}} - E_{\\text{reactants}}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $E_{\\text{peak}}$: the potential energy at the top of the barrier, level Q.\n  * $E_{\\text{reactants}}$: the potential energy at the start of the forward reaction, level P.\n* **Why we use it:** Activation energy is the energy needed to go from the reactants up to the peak, so we find the difference $Q - P$.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Use the reactant level for the forward reaction; don’t subtract from the product level."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "step_walkthrough",
    "final_answer": "D",
