{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "A reaction reaches equilibrium in a closed container according to the following balanced equation:\n\n$3\\text{H}_2(\\text{g}) + \\text{N}_2(\\text{g}) \\rightleftharpoons 2\\text{NH}_3(\\text{g}) \\quad \\Delta H < 0$\n\nWhich ONE of the following changes will INCREASE the value of the equilibrium constant?\nA Removing $\\text{NH}_3(\\text{g})$\nB Heating the container\nC Cooling the container\nD Increasing the volume of the container"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The key is the negative enthalpy change: the forward reaction releases heat. Use a familiar two-way route analogy to diagnose whether the learner has the sign right before selecting an option.",
      "content": "Think of the forward reaction like a two-way taxi route that delivers ammonia and also releases heat. Since $\\Delta H < 0$, does the forward reaction release heat or absorb it?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I think it absorbs heat, so heating the container (B) should make more ammonia faster."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner has reversed the meaning of $\\Delta H < 0$ and is mixing up reaction rate with the equilibrium constant. Correct the sign, map heat to the products in the taxi-route analogy, and distinguish temperature effects from changes in concentration or volume.",
      "content": "Here the sign is the important clue: $\\Delta H < 0$ means the forward reaction **releases** heat, so heat acts like another product on the forward taxi route. Cooling removes heat, and the equilibrium shifts towards products to replace it. Therefore, the equilibrium constant increases when the container is cooled.\n\n**Final Answer: C — Cooling the container.** Heating may change how quickly equilibrium is reached, but it does not increase $K$ for this exothermic reaction.\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $K_c = \\dfrac{[\\text{NH}_3]^2}{[\\text{H}_2]^3[\\text{N}_2]}$\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $K_c$: the equilibrium constant, showing the ratio of product to reactant concentrations at equilibrium.\n  * $[\\text{NH}_3]$, $[\\text{H}_2]$ and $[\\text{N}_2]$: the equilibrium concentrations of ammonia, hydrogen and nitrogen; the powers come from the balanced equation.\n* **Why we use it:** Temperature changes the equilibrium position and therefore the equilibrium concentrations in this expression. Because the forward reaction releases heat, cooling favours ammonia and increases $K_c$. In the taxi analogy, cooling is like removing heat from the destination side, so the balance shifts towards making more products.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Only temperature changes the value of the equilibrium constant. Removing a substance or changing the volume can shift the equilibrium position, but does not change $K_c$."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "analogy_real_world",
    "final_answer": "C",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nK = sp.Symbol('K')\nanswer = 'C'  # Cooling favours products for an exothermic forward reaction, increasing K.\nprint(answer)",
    "techniques": [
      "le_chateliers_principle",
      "temperature_dependence_of_equilibrium_constant"
    ]
  }
}

{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Sulphuric acid ionises in water according to these equations:\n\n$\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4(\\ell) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(\\ell) \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+(\\text{aq}) + \\text{HSO}_4^-(\\text{aq})$\n\n$\\text{HSO}_4^-(\\text{aq}) + \\text{H}_2\\text{O}(\\ell) \\rightleftharpoons \\text{H}_3\\text{O}^+(\\text{aq}) + \\text{SO}_4^{2-}(\\text{aq})$\n\nWhich statements are true?\nI: $\\text{H}_2\\text{O}(\\ell)$ acts as a base in both reactions.\nII: $\\text{HSO}_4^-(\\text{aq})$ acts as an ampholyte.\nIII: $\\text{SO}_4^{2-}(\\text{aq})$ is the conjugate base of $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$.\n\nA I only\nB I and II\nC I and III\nD I, II and III"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner needs to distinguish proton acceptance, ampholytes, and conjugate pairs. Start with one focused diagnostic question about proton transfer without evaluating the options for them.",
      "content": "Which species accepts a proton in each reaction, and what does that tell you about water’s role?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "I think it is D. Water accepts a proton, and sulfate is the conjugate base of sulphuric acid because it is the final product."
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "The learner correctly identifies water as a proton acceptor, but assumes the final product is automatically the conjugate base of the starting acid. Clarify that conjugate pairs differ by one proton, then assess each statement and give the concise answer.",
      "content": "Water accepts a proton in both reactions, so I is true. An ampholyte can both donate and accept a proton: $\\text{HSO}_4^-$ can do both, so II is true. The conjugate base of $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$ after it loses one proton is $\\text{HSO}_4^-$; $\\text{SO}_4^{2-}$ is the conjugate base of $\\text{HSO}_4^-$. So III is false.\n\n**Final Answer: B — I and II**\n\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $\\mathrm{HA + H_2O \\rightleftharpoons H_3O^+ + A^-}$\n* **What the symbols mean:**\n  * $\\mathrm{HA}$: an acid that donates a proton.\n  * $\\mathrm{H_2O}$: water, which accepts the proton here.\n  * $\\mathrm{H_3O^+}$: hydronium, formed when water accepts a proton.\n  * $\\mathrm{A^-}$: the conjugate base left after the acid loses a proton.\n* **Why we use it:** A conjugate acid–base pair differs by one proton. This helps identify that $\\text{HSO}_4^-$ is the conjugate base formed from $\\text{H}_2\\text{SO}_4$ in the first reaction.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Check whether a proposed conjugate pair differs by exactly one $\\text{H}^+$. Don’t identify the conjugate base just by looking for the final product."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "socratic_hints",
    "final_answer": "B",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\nstatements = [sp.true, sp.true, sp.false]\noptions = {(True, True, False): \"B\"}\nprint(options[tuple(bool(s) for s in statements)])",
    "techniques": [
