{
  "messages": [
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Which ONE of the following reactions, when used in a voltaic cell, will give a positive reading on the voltmeter?<br>A $\\text{Mg}^{2+}(\\text{aq}) + \\text{Zn}(\\text{s}) \\rightarrow \\text{Mg}(\\text{s}) + \\text{Zn}^{2+}(\\text{aq})$<br>B $\\text{Cu}(\\text{s}) + 2\\text{Ag}^+(\\text{aq}) \\rightarrow \\text{Cu}^{2+}(\\text{aq}) + 2\\text{Ag}(\\text{s})$<br>C $\\text{Co}^{2+}(\\text{aq}) + \\text{Sn}^{2+}(\\text{aq}) \\rightarrow \\text{Co}(\\\text{s}) + \\text{Sn}^{4+}(\\text{aq})$<br>D $3\\text{Ni}^{2+}(\\text{aq}) + 2\\text{Fe}(\\text{s}) \\rightarrow 3\\text{Ni}(\\text{s}) + 2\\text{Fe}^{3+}(\\text{aq})$"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Introduce a helpful mnemonic to connect voltmeter reading (cell potential) with spontaneity. Ask a diagnostic question about what a positive reading means. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"0.80 - 0.34\") -> 0.46]",
      "content": "Let's unpack what a positive voltmeter reading actually means in a voltaic cell! 🔋\n\nIn electrochemistry, we can use a quick cognitive anchor: **SP** for **S**pontaneous = **P**ositive cell potential ($E^\\circ_{\text{cell}} > 0$). \n\nLooking at the options, we need to find which reaction happens naturally on its own. Do you remember what rule we use with standard reduction potentials to tell if a redox reaction is spontaneous?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "is it where the reducing agent is higher up than the oxidizing agent on the table?"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Validate student's mention of the table position rule. Guide them to apply it specifically to Option B using reduction potentials. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"0.80 - 0.34\") -> 0.46]",
      "content": "Checking the Table of Standard Reduction Potentials: a spontaneous reaction occurs precisely when the reducing agent (the substance losing electrons, found on the right) is **above** the oxidizing agent (the substance gaining electrons, found on the left).\n\nLet's test this using option B: $\\text{Cu}(\\text{s})$ and $\\text{Ag}^+(\\text{aq})$. Look up $\\text{Cu}^{2+}/\\text{Cu}$ and $\\text{Ag}^+/\\text{Ag}$ on your data table. Which one is higher up as a reducing agent on the right side?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Ag+ is lower down, and Cu is higher up on the right. So Cu can reduce Ag+!"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "reasoning": "Confirm correctness, conclude with final answer B, and provide formula breakdown and exam tip as required. [TOOL CHECK: calculator(expression=\"0.80 - 0.34\") -> 0.46]",
      "content": "Copper ($\\text{Cu}$) is higher up on the right as a reducing agent than silver ions ($\\text{Ag}^+$) on the left, making this reaction spontaneous with a positive cell potential! 🎉\n\n**Final Answer:** B\n\n---\n### 📖 Formula Breakdown (How It Works):\n* **The Formula:** $E^\\circ_{\text{cell}} = E^\\circ_{\text{cathode}} - E^\\circ_{\text{anode}}$ (or $E^\\circ_{\text{reduction}} - E^\\circ_{\text{oxidation}}$)\n* **What the letters mean:**\n  * $E^\\circ_{\text{cell}}$ is the total standard cell potential (measured in Volts, $\\text{V}$)\n  * $E^\\circ_{\text{cathode}}$ is the reduction potential of the substance being reduced\n  * $E^\\circ_{\text{anode}}$ is the reduction potential of the substance being oxidized\n* **Why we use it:** A voltaic cell converts chemical energy into electrical energy spontaneously. For the voltmeter to show a positive reading, the calculated cell potential must be positive ($> 0$), confirming that the forward reaction happens naturally without external energy.\n\n🎯 **Exam Tip:** Always check your Table of Standard Reduction Potentials carefully! If the reducing agent on the right is above the oxidizing agent on the left, the reaction is spontaneous and $E^\\circ_{\text{cell}}$ will be positive."
    }
  ],
  "meta": {
    "strategy_used": "mnemonic",
    "final_answer": "B",
    "verify_code": "import sympy as sp\ne_ag = sp.Rational(80, 100)\ne_cu = sp.Rational(34, 100)\ne_cell = e_ag - e_cu\nprint(f\"E_cell = {float(e_cell)} V (Positive -> Spontaneous -> B)\")",
    "techniques": [
      "mnemonic",
      "units_check",
      "table_reference"
    ]
  }
}

{
