      "reasoning": "The learner applies the cathode-positive rule without distinguishing cell types. Use a powered-device analogy to focus on the external power supply in an electrolytic cell, then ask one diagnostic question without stating the option.",
      "content": "Let’s check that rule using a familiar idea: an electrolytic cell is like a device being charged from an external power supply. The supply drives electrons around, so the electrode signs depend on how the cell is connected to that supply.\n\nIf the power supply pushes electrons **into** an electrode, would that electrode be connected to the supply’s positive terminal or its negative terminal?"
    },
    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "The negative terminal, because it supplies electrons. So electrons enter the cathode, which means the cathode is negative in an electrolytic cell. Then oxidation must happen at the anode, so I think A is correct."
    },
    {
